WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.500
The Adventures of Ozzie and Harriet, starring the entire Nelson family.

00:04.500 --> 00:07.500
Here is Ozzie, who plays the part of Ozzie Nelson.

00:07.500 --> 00:11.000
And of course, his lovely wife Harriet, as Harriet Nelson.

00:11.000 --> 00:15.000
The older of the Nelson boys, David, appears as David Nelson.

00:15.000 --> 00:19.500
And his younger brother, the irrepressible Ricky, played by Ricky Nelson.

00:19.500 --> 00:23.500
The Nelsons next door neighbor, Thorny, is played by Don DeFore.

00:23.500 --> 00:25.500
Who's that in the closet?

00:25.500 --> 00:27.500
Don't be scared, Mom. It's me, your son, Ricky.

00:27.500 --> 00:29.500
Oh, Ricky, get up and take that skeleton suit off.

00:29.500 --> 00:32.500
You'll have it worn out by the time Halloween gets here.

00:32.500 --> 00:34.500
I'm just looking for my skeleton hand.

00:34.500 --> 00:36.500
Well, you won't find them in there.

00:36.500 --> 00:39.500
You come on, get up and take that suit off. It gives me the willies anyway.

00:39.500 --> 00:40.500
Oh, gee.

00:40.500 --> 00:41.500
What's the trouble?

00:41.500 --> 00:43.500
Well, take a look on the floor of the closet.

00:43.500 --> 00:46.500
Well, is there a dog in there?

00:46.500 --> 00:48.500
No, you better take another look.

00:48.500 --> 00:50.500
That's the only thing that's in there.

00:50.500 --> 00:53.500
Is there a dog in there?

00:53.500 --> 00:55.500
No, you better take another look.

00:55.500 --> 00:57.500
That happens to be your small son.

00:57.500 --> 00:59.500
Well, how about that?

00:59.500 --> 01:03.500
I could have sworn some dog left his bones in there.

01:03.500 --> 01:05.500
Me, out there, huh, Pop?

01:05.500 --> 01:07.500
Yes, you're scary, all right.

01:07.500 --> 01:09.500
Aren't you rushing the season a little?

01:09.500 --> 01:11.500
Halloween isn't quite here.

01:11.500 --> 01:13.500
I'm just getting all my bones together.

01:13.500 --> 01:15.500
I thought they were painted on the suit.

01:15.500 --> 01:17.500
Oh, no, the hands come separate.

01:17.500 --> 01:20.500
Well, sure. I guess you've never seen a real-life skeleton.

01:20.500 --> 01:23.500
No, come to think of it, I guess I never have.

01:23.500 --> 01:25.500
And I'll gladly forego the pleasure, thank you.

01:25.500 --> 01:26.500
You're welcome.

01:26.500 --> 01:28.500
It's a neat outfit, huh, Pop?

01:28.500 --> 01:30.500
Yeah, like I said, it's plenty scary, boy.

01:30.500 --> 01:32.500
Do you think anybody will recognize me?

01:32.500 --> 01:34.500
Well, you sure had me fooled.

01:34.500 --> 01:35.500
Hi, Mom.

01:35.500 --> 01:36.500
Hi, Pop.

01:36.500 --> 01:38.500
David, guess who I am.

01:38.500 --> 01:41.500
Oh, brother.

01:41.500 --> 01:44.500
Why, how'd he get?

01:44.500 --> 01:46.500
I'm sure he did.

01:46.500 --> 01:48.500
What have you got there, dear?

01:48.500 --> 01:50.500
Oh, just some stuff for a Halloween party at school.

01:50.500 --> 01:51.500
I'm the treasurer.

01:51.500 --> 01:54.500
Oh, I didn't know you were going to have a Halloween party at school.

01:54.500 --> 01:56.500
Well, we're just going to dress up and play some games.

01:56.500 --> 01:57.500
Oh, that sounds like fun.

01:57.500 --> 01:59.500
School parties always are.

01:59.500 --> 02:01.500
You said it. We get out of study.

02:01.500 --> 02:03.500
Yeah, Halloween's a lot of fun for kids.

02:03.500 --> 02:05.500
And don't you like Halloween, Pop?

02:05.500 --> 02:08.500
Oh, sure, but you know, as you get a little bit older,

02:08.500 --> 02:10.500
you get kind of tired of that stuff.

02:10.500 --> 02:12.500
You mean going to parties and playing games and stuff?

02:12.500 --> 02:15.500
Yeah, see, there are certain traditional and seasonal games,

02:15.500 --> 02:18.500
like pin the tail on the donkey and ducking for apples,

02:18.500 --> 02:21.500
and they get a little stale as the years go by.

02:21.500 --> 02:23.500
We make up a lot of new stuff, Pop.

02:23.500 --> 02:25.500
What do you mean make up new stuff?

02:25.500 --> 02:27.500
We bring the old-fashioned games up to date.

02:27.500 --> 02:29.500
Oh, that sounds interesting.

02:29.500 --> 02:31.500
How do you bring pin the tail on the donkey up to date?

02:31.500 --> 02:33.500
Or shouldn't I ask?

02:33.500 --> 02:35.500
Oh, sure.

02:35.500 --> 02:38.500
You draw a big picture of the teacher's face on the blackboard.

02:38.500 --> 02:40.500
Oh, I don't get the picture.

02:40.500 --> 02:41.500
Well, then we play the game.

02:41.500 --> 02:43.500
And what do you call it?

02:43.500 --> 02:45.500
The mustache on the teacher.

02:45.500 --> 02:47.500
Well, I haven't much the teacher's going to walk in on these days.

02:47.500 --> 02:49.500
You'll have a new game.

02:49.500 --> 02:54.500
I am not Salvador Dali on the blackboard 50 times.

02:54.500 --> 02:55.500
Oh, no, Pop.

02:55.500 --> 02:57.500
Our teacher was the one who made up the game.

02:57.500 --> 03:00.500
You know, sometimes this modern psychology puzzles me a little.

03:00.500 --> 03:04.500
They call it relieving suppressed desires.

03:04.500 --> 03:06.500
Yeah, I guess it does it best.

03:06.500 --> 03:07.500
How's it seem to work?

03:07.500 --> 03:09.500
Heck, I don't even know what it means.

03:09.500 --> 03:12.500
It's always a part of the psychology.

03:12.500 --> 03:14.500
You and Mom going to a Halloween party this year, Pop?

03:14.500 --> 03:18.500
Yeah, I suppose the girls will get together and whip up some sort of a deal at the last minute.

03:18.500 --> 03:19.500
They usually do.

03:19.500 --> 03:22.500
Gives them an excuse to dress up a bit.

03:22.500 --> 03:25.500
Well, as a matter of fact, we were talking about that yesterday.

03:25.500 --> 03:27.500
And I think we'll skip it this year.

03:27.500 --> 03:30.500
Well, I'm sure you won't get any objection from the men.

03:30.500 --> 03:34.500
Personally, I think it's about time we gave Halloween back to the kids anyway.

03:34.500 --> 03:36.500
Well, it'll save us a lot of trouble.

03:36.500 --> 03:40.500
Trying to drag you guys out of the house to a party is no fun either.

03:40.500 --> 03:42.500
I like going to parties.

03:42.500 --> 03:44.500
Oh, boy!

03:44.500 --> 03:46.500
Hey, stay out of that, Ricky.

03:46.500 --> 03:47.500
Hey, what is this white stuff?

03:47.500 --> 03:49.500
That's paint for the faces.

03:49.500 --> 03:51.500
Hey, neat, boy.

03:51.500 --> 03:53.500
Leave it alone. That's for the party at school.

03:53.500 --> 03:56.500
You put this on my face and I'll look just like a skeleton.

03:56.500 --> 03:57.500
Hey, cut that. Let me see it.

03:57.500 --> 04:01.500
Oh, gee, Pop, that's for the party at school. I bought it with the class money.

04:01.500 --> 04:03.500
That's okay. I'll only use a little of it.

04:03.500 --> 04:05.500
Gee, I can see the headlines now.

04:05.500 --> 04:09.500
Boy uses school funds to turn brother into skeleton.

04:09.500 --> 04:11.500
I'll give you a little extra change if you need it.

04:11.500 --> 04:13.500
Why am I going to look spooky?

04:13.500 --> 04:15.500
I hope that stuff comes off.

04:15.500 --> 04:16.500
It's supposed to.

04:20.500 --> 04:22.500
It's a pretty good paint job, I think.

04:25.500 --> 04:27.500
We've got some black stuff here, too, Pop, if you want it.

04:27.500 --> 04:30.500
Oh, yeah. Hey, that's swell. We could use this for the eyes.

04:30.500 --> 04:32.500
Look at this, Harriet.

04:32.500 --> 04:36.500
I never thought I'd see the day when I'd sit here and let your father blacken your eyes.

04:36.500 --> 04:39.500
How do you like that? Eyes, nose, and mouth.

04:39.500 --> 04:42.500
Yeah, that sounds like the usual arrangement.

04:43.500 --> 04:45.500
I'd look at myself.

04:47.500 --> 04:49.500
Oh, boy, do I look gruesome.

04:49.500 --> 04:50.500
Pretty neat job, eh?

04:50.500 --> 04:53.500
Yeah. We're working our hind till Halloween.

04:53.500 --> 04:56.500
I think you're dunking your head into a nice bowl of soap and water.

04:56.500 --> 04:59.500
You can put that word paint on again when the time comes.

04:59.500 --> 05:02.500
I just want to leave it on till I rehearse my theme song.

05:02.500 --> 05:03.500
Your theme song?

05:03.500 --> 05:04.500
Sure.

05:04.500 --> 05:07.500
The ankle bone connected to the shin bone.

05:18.500 --> 05:19.500
Hi, Oz.

05:19.500 --> 05:20.500
Oh, hi, Ernie.

05:20.500 --> 05:22.500
Hey, what do you do with the broom?

05:22.500 --> 05:25.500
Well, you know, Halloween, once a year, you usually celebrate.

05:25.500 --> 05:27.500
Holy smoke, what's wrong with your hand?

05:27.500 --> 05:29.500
It's all white. Looks a little undernourished.

05:29.500 --> 05:32.500
Oh, no, I just put a little Halloween makeup on Ricky's face.

05:32.500 --> 05:35.500
Kids are going to go out to Trick or Treat tomorrow night,

05:35.500 --> 05:37.500
and he's going to dress up as a skeleton.

05:37.500 --> 05:40.500
Oh, boy, Halloween is sure a lot of fun for the kids.

05:40.500 --> 05:41.500
Yeah, it really is.

05:41.500 --> 05:43.500
Gives them a chance to let off a little steam.

05:43.500 --> 05:46.500
As a matter of fact, we've had some pretty good parties the last few years.

05:46.500 --> 05:48.500
Well, they've been okay.

05:48.500 --> 05:52.500
Personally, I still think Halloween is mainly a night for kids, though.

05:52.500 --> 05:55.500
Come to think of it, I don't think I've heard anything about this year's party.

05:55.500 --> 05:57.500
What are the girls' plans, do you know?

05:57.500 --> 06:00.500
Oh, from what I understand, they're not going to give a party this year.

06:00.500 --> 06:03.500
Oh, that's a shame. Don't you think we could talk them into it?

06:03.500 --> 06:06.500
Well, are you kidding? Let's leave well enough alone.

06:06.500 --> 06:12.500
Don't tell me you actually enjoy those horrible fiascos with those corny games and those idiotic costumes.

06:12.500 --> 06:14.500
Well, there's a lot of trouble with that.

06:14.500 --> 06:17.500
Well, you know Catherine spent a whole week making that outfit I wore last year?

06:17.500 --> 06:19.500
You mean at the Dunkles?

06:19.500 --> 06:21.500
No, that was the party before last.

06:21.500 --> 06:23.500
Last year's party was at the Randolphs, don't you remember?

06:23.500 --> 06:25.500
Oh, yeah, that's right. Yeah.

06:25.500 --> 06:28.500
Sure, that was the year I won first prize for the best costume.

06:28.500 --> 06:30.500
You won the first prize?

06:30.500 --> 06:33.500
Sure, you remember. I wore that devil's outfit.

06:33.500 --> 06:36.500
I thought you came dressed up as a fireman.

06:36.500 --> 06:42.500
Sure you did. Don't you remember you insisted you could jump out of the second story window into the big apple tree?

06:42.500 --> 06:44.500
Oh, that's a good thing, isn't it?

06:44.500 --> 06:46.500
And I darn near made it, too.

06:48.500 --> 06:50.500
No, I'm sure it was Halloween.

06:50.500 --> 06:53.500
Certainly, we were ducking for apples and somebody spiked the water.

06:53.500 --> 06:56.500
You know, we've had some pretty good Halloween parties at that.

06:56.500 --> 07:01.500
Yeah, I never will forget the night that Mrs. O'Shaughnessy put that big sheet over her head and she said,

07:01.500 --> 07:03.500
I am the devil.

07:03.500 --> 07:05.500
I don't remember that.

07:05.500 --> 07:07.500
Sure you do. O'Shaughnessy didn't know who it was.

07:07.500 --> 07:10.500
He said, shake hands with me, I married your sister.

07:14.500 --> 07:19.500
You know, I think the least the wives could have done was to ask us before they called off this year's party.

07:19.500 --> 07:21.500
Oh, I don't think it's been called off.

07:21.500 --> 07:23.500
Definitely, it's just they haven't made any plans for it.

07:23.500 --> 07:27.500
Well, right there is the reason why a lot of the parties haven't been so good.

07:27.500 --> 07:28.500
What do you mean?

07:28.500 --> 07:30.500
Well, they haven't been properly organized.

07:30.500 --> 07:33.500
You can't just give a party haphazardly, you've got to organize it.

07:33.500 --> 07:36.500
You've got to plan the whole evening, hour by hour, step by step.

07:36.500 --> 07:38.500
Especially the entertainment.

07:38.500 --> 07:40.500
And by somebody who has a little talent for that sort of thing.

07:40.500 --> 07:42.500
That's exactly what I mean.

07:42.500 --> 07:47.500
So you know what we could do, as if we wanted to turn this into a party tomorrow night, say, for instance,

07:47.500 --> 07:51.500
is to work out a schedule for the entire evening.

07:51.500 --> 07:54.500
Exactly what games we're going to play, what time we play them,

07:54.500 --> 07:57.500
exactly what time we eat, what time we dance,

07:57.500 --> 08:00.500
and that way we'd have the entire evening under control.

08:00.500 --> 08:02.500
Now that's the sensible way to do it.

08:02.500 --> 08:05.500
And instead of having everyone dressed up in costumes and causing a lot of confusion,

08:05.500 --> 08:09.500
we could just have a couple of more talented fellas dressed up to keep things going.

08:09.500 --> 08:12.500
But wearing really clever costumes.

08:12.500 --> 08:13.500
That's right.

08:13.500 --> 08:16.500
So much better to have a couple of fellas dressed up in costumes.

08:16.500 --> 08:19.500
Much better to have a couple of fellas with real talent,

08:19.500 --> 08:22.500
rather than a whole room full of would-be comedians.

08:22.500 --> 08:24.500
Rather short notice.

08:24.500 --> 08:27.500
What do you think we ought to wear?

08:27.500 --> 08:28.500
What do you think we ought to do with it?

08:28.500 --> 08:29.500
Are you kidding us?

08:29.500 --> 08:31.500
This sounds great to me.

08:31.500 --> 08:33.500
I could dig up that devil's outfit I wore last year.

08:33.500 --> 08:35.500
Say, I have a devil's outfit up in the attic, too.

08:35.500 --> 08:37.500
We could put on a big sign,

08:37.500 --> 08:39.500
Aren't we devils?

08:39.500 --> 08:42.500
No, no.

08:42.500 --> 08:44.500
Costumes, we could think of something else.

08:44.500 --> 08:47.500
One thing for sure, the entire evening will go off smoothly.

08:47.500 --> 08:49.500
I mean, none of this haphazard stuff.

08:49.500 --> 08:52.500
It'll all be planned step by step.

08:52.500 --> 08:54.500
Do you think the girls will like the idea?

08:54.500 --> 08:56.500
I mean, us taking it out of their hands this way?

08:56.500 --> 08:58.500
Well, they'll be delighted.

08:58.500 --> 08:59.500
As a matter of fact, the more I think of it,

08:59.500 --> 09:04.500
I'll bet that's why they were stalling around, waiting for us to take over.

09:04.500 --> 09:05.500
Boy, I could see us right now,

09:05.500 --> 09:10.500
in our mad costumes, running around, taking charge of everything.

09:10.500 --> 09:13.500
You know, Thorny, I think there must be a trace of genius in our families.

09:13.500 --> 09:15.500
Oh, there's no doubt about it.

09:15.500 --> 09:21.500
A definite trace of genius with a faint flavoring of ham.

09:21.500 --> 09:25.500
You know, Thorny and I have whipped up what we think is a pretty darn good idea.

09:25.500 --> 09:26.500
Oh, what is it?

09:26.500 --> 09:28.500
Well, you know how each year around Halloween,

09:28.500 --> 09:32.500
you gals sort of throw together a little impromptu party?

09:32.500 --> 09:34.500
And you don't want us to give one this year.

09:34.500 --> 09:36.500
You said that before.

09:36.500 --> 09:38.500
Well, they've been pleasant enough little affairs.

09:38.500 --> 09:41.500
Everybody's had a pretty good time.

09:41.500 --> 09:46.500
But the big fault has been that nobody has taken the trouble to plan them correctly.

09:46.500 --> 09:48.500
But this year, the whole thing's going to be different.

09:48.500 --> 09:52.500
It's Thornberry and Nelson to the rescue.

09:52.500 --> 09:54.500
May I ask a question?

09:54.500 --> 09:57.500
No, you're not even allowed to ask any questions.

09:57.500 --> 10:00.500
Thorny and I are going to take charge of all the operations.

10:00.500 --> 10:03.500
You gals are just allowed to relax and enjoy yourselves.

10:03.500 --> 10:05.500
Oh, fine. That sounds like a wonderful idea.

10:05.500 --> 10:08.500
You know, I hate to brag, but I really think, Harry,

10:08.500 --> 10:11.500
this year's Halloween party is going to be the best one we've ever given.

10:11.500 --> 10:12.500
And why do you ask?

10:12.500 --> 10:14.500
I'm not allowed to ask questions.

10:14.500 --> 10:16.500
No, no. Well, okay.

10:16.500 --> 10:19.500
Because this is going to be a well-planned party.

10:19.500 --> 10:22.500
And a well-planned party is a successful party.

10:22.500 --> 10:24.500
Thank you all so much for that.

10:24.500 --> 10:27.500
They'll take care of the little things, and the big things will take care of themselves.

10:27.500 --> 10:29.500
Yes, that's very true.

10:29.500 --> 10:33.500
Now, first of all, we figured we'd work out a complete schedule for all the games.

10:33.500 --> 10:36.500
You can notice right from the start that we're going to plan everything

10:36.500 --> 10:39.500
with typical masculine efficiency.

10:39.500 --> 10:40.500
Pencil, please.

10:40.500 --> 10:41.500
Here's your pencil.

10:41.500 --> 10:43.500
Oh, yes, I found it.

10:43.500 --> 10:46.500
Now, first of all, we thought of quotations.

10:46.500 --> 10:47.500
That's all.

10:47.500 --> 10:49.500
Well, bring plenty of paper and pencil.

10:49.500 --> 10:50.500
All right.

10:50.500 --> 10:54.500
We start the evening off with, say, quotations.

10:54.500 --> 11:02.500
We play that from, say, 8.30 to 9.30.

11:02.500 --> 11:05.500
See, knowing the time you want to start a game, you want to finish it,

11:05.500 --> 11:10.500
that's one of those little details that make the whole party flow along smoothly.

11:10.500 --> 11:17.500
Then, say, at 9.30, we'll bob for apples.

11:17.500 --> 11:18.500
That's always fun.

11:18.500 --> 11:23.500
Oh, please, ask them not to wear any hair oil this time.

11:23.500 --> 11:28.500
Okay, no hair oil on the apple ducking.

11:28.500 --> 11:31.500
And we do that for, say, 15 minutes.

11:31.500 --> 11:34.500
Oh, make sure you've got a clean shirt to wear, too.

11:34.500 --> 11:37.500
Oh, I'll be wearing a costume.

11:37.500 --> 11:38.500
A costume?

11:38.500 --> 11:41.500
You mean everybody has to get a costume at the last minute?

11:41.500 --> 11:43.500
Gee, I don't know quite how to explain this.

11:43.500 --> 11:45.500
This is one of the little surprises we had planned.

11:45.500 --> 11:47.500
Well, you'll probably find out about it.

11:47.500 --> 11:50.500
You see, you know how each year everybody gets together

11:50.500 --> 11:54.500
and just everybody puts on a silly costume and makes a darn fool of himself?

11:54.500 --> 11:59.500
Well, this year, just Thorny and I are going to do it.

11:59.500 --> 12:02.500
I mean, just Thorny and I are going to wear the costumes.

12:02.500 --> 12:04.500
What about the rest of us?

12:04.500 --> 12:07.500
Well, you just wear your regular clothes.

12:07.500 --> 12:09.500
Let's see, where was I? Oh, oh, here.

12:09.500 --> 12:11.500
Well, let's allow, say, uh...

12:11.500 --> 12:12.500
Oh, here.

12:20.500 --> 12:21.500
Hi, Bob.

12:21.500 --> 12:22.500
Hi, Bob.

12:22.500 --> 12:23.500
Oh, hi, boys.

12:23.500 --> 12:24.500
What are you doing?

12:24.500 --> 12:28.500
Just checking over this list of things we're going to do at the party tonight.

12:28.500 --> 12:30.500
Mr. Thornberry and I are planning all the details,

12:30.500 --> 12:32.500
you know, all the entertainment and stuff like that.

12:32.500 --> 12:34.500
What kind of entertainment are you going to have?

12:34.500 --> 12:37.500
Well, that's what I'm checking over now.

12:37.500 --> 12:40.500
You boys know the secret of a successful party?

12:40.500 --> 12:43.500
Sure. Plenty of ice cream and cake, boy.

12:43.500 --> 12:44.500
Oh, yes, refreshments are important,

12:44.500 --> 12:47.500
but the main thing is to plan the party carefully.

12:47.500 --> 12:50.500
A well-planned party is a successful party.

12:50.500 --> 12:51.500
Always remember that.

12:51.500 --> 12:53.500
You saw my skeleton outfit, didn't you, Pop?

12:53.500 --> 12:55.500
Oh, yeah, it's real neat.

12:55.500 --> 12:56.500
Yeah, we've all seen it.

12:56.500 --> 12:58.500
You've been wearing it around here for the past week.

12:58.500 --> 13:00.500
It makes you look real thin.

13:00.500 --> 13:01.500
People feel sorry for you,

13:01.500 --> 13:05.500
and that way you get more ice cream and cake.

13:05.500 --> 13:06.500
How's the party coming, dear?

13:06.500 --> 13:07.500
Oh, hi.

13:07.500 --> 13:10.500
Well, I've got it just about all planned.

13:10.500 --> 13:12.500
I thought we'd finish off the evening with a little dancing.

13:12.500 --> 13:13.500
Dancing?

13:13.500 --> 13:16.500
I hope they don't pull that stuff at our party, boy.

13:16.500 --> 13:17.500
What are you worried about?

13:17.500 --> 13:18.500
You can't dance anyway.

13:18.500 --> 13:20.500
You can, I suppose.

13:20.500 --> 13:22.500
Look, why don't you guys go outside for a while, huh?

13:22.500 --> 13:24.500
I've got a lot of work I want to do here.

13:24.500 --> 13:26.500
I think I'll put on my skeleton outfit.

13:26.500 --> 13:28.500
Come on, boom boy.

13:32.500 --> 13:34.500
You have a novelty dance at one.

13:34.500 --> 13:35.500
That'll be fun.

13:35.500 --> 13:37.500
You know, husbands all dance with their own wives.

13:40.500 --> 13:41.500
Records.

13:41.500 --> 13:43.500
Better decide on the records.

13:43.500 --> 13:45.500
You're really taking care of everything, aren't you?

13:45.500 --> 13:47.500
Yeah, well, the records are very important.

13:47.500 --> 13:48.500
Hi, folks.

13:48.500 --> 13:49.500
Oh, hi, Ernie.

13:49.500 --> 13:52.500
The back door was open, so I barged right in.

13:52.500 --> 13:53.500
Well, good for you.

13:53.500 --> 13:55.500
What have you got in the box there?

13:55.500 --> 13:58.500
Well, I can show you, Oz, but Harriet will have to leave the room.

13:58.500 --> 14:00.500
It's a surprise for tonight.

14:00.500 --> 14:01.500
Well, come on, Harriet.

14:01.500 --> 14:02.500
You heard the man.

14:02.500 --> 14:03.500
OK, I'm leaving.

14:03.500 --> 14:04.500
I know what I'm not wanted.

14:09.500 --> 14:10.500
Well, come on.

14:10.500 --> 14:11.500
Let's see the big secret.

14:11.500 --> 14:12.500
Here, Oz.

14:12.500 --> 14:14.500
Take a look for yourself.

14:14.500 --> 14:17.500
What's this, new seat covers for the car?

14:17.500 --> 14:19.500
Oz, you know Sandy McTavish over at the Lodge?

14:19.500 --> 14:20.500
Yeah.

14:20.500 --> 14:22.500
Well, he let me borrow the Scotch outfit.

14:22.500 --> 14:23.500
Oh, how about this?

14:23.500 --> 14:25.500
Make a terrific costume for the party.

14:25.500 --> 14:26.500
Oh, this is wonderful, Thorny.

14:26.500 --> 14:28.500
Yeah, I left the bagpipes at home.

14:28.500 --> 14:30.500
Got bagpipes and everything, huh?

14:30.500 --> 14:31.500
Oh, smirkly.

14:31.500 --> 14:33.500
You ought to hear me wheeze out Annie Laurie.

14:33.500 --> 14:34.500
How about this?

14:34.500 --> 14:35.500
This is wonderful.

14:35.500 --> 14:37.500
Say, by the way, have you got the program all set?

14:37.500 --> 14:40.500
Well, yeah, I just, oh, I'm glad you reminded me of that.

14:40.500 --> 14:41.500
Here.

14:41.500 --> 14:43.500
I have a few items here that we need for tonight.

14:43.500 --> 14:46.500
I thought maybe you'd run down the store and pick them up, OK?

14:46.500 --> 14:48.500
Say, what am I, the errand boy?

14:48.500 --> 14:50.500
Well, you've got to do some of the work, too, Thorny.

14:50.500 --> 14:52.500
Just a short list.

14:52.500 --> 14:54.500
Oh, and here, we need some oranges here.

14:54.500 --> 14:55.500
Maybe about two dozen.

14:55.500 --> 14:58.500
I thought we'd play that orange game where you tuck it under your chin.

14:58.500 --> 15:01.500
And then get some comic prizes for the game.

15:01.500 --> 15:02.500
There's somebody at the door.

15:02.500 --> 15:03.500
You'd better get this stuff out of here.

15:03.500 --> 15:04.500
Comic prizes for the games.

15:04.500 --> 15:06.500
You know, just long noses, anything funny.

15:06.500 --> 15:08.500
Just use your own judgment, OK?

15:08.500 --> 15:09.500
Yeah, OK.

15:09.500 --> 15:10.500
I'll go see you later.

15:10.500 --> 15:11.500
And don't let Captain see the costume.

15:11.500 --> 15:12.500
Oh, boy.

15:12.500 --> 15:13.500
Just a moment.

15:13.500 --> 15:15.500
Just a moment.

15:20.500 --> 15:22.500
Well, what have we here?

15:22.500 --> 15:23.500
Trick or treat.

15:23.500 --> 15:24.500
Trick or treat.

15:24.500 --> 15:27.500
Oh, well, you look like a couple of desperate characters.

15:27.500 --> 15:29.500
I'm afraid I'll have to give in.

15:29.500 --> 15:33.500
I just happen to have a couple of lollipops here.

15:33.500 --> 15:36.500
OK, there you are.

15:36.500 --> 15:37.500
Happy Halloween.

15:37.500 --> 15:39.500
Happy Halloween.

15:39.500 --> 15:45.500
Hey, wait for me.

15:45.500 --> 15:46.500
Who was that?

15:46.500 --> 15:48.500
Oh, just a couple of neighborhood kids at that trick or treat stuff.

15:48.500 --> 15:50.500
You should have seen them, the cutest little guys.

15:50.500 --> 15:51.500
Oh.

15:51.500 --> 15:53.500
Say, listen, if you've got just a minute, if there's just one question,

15:53.500 --> 15:55.500
I'd like to ask you about the party tonight.

15:55.500 --> 15:56.500
Harriet, please.

15:56.500 --> 15:59.500
I told you before, you're not allowed to ask any questions at all.

15:59.500 --> 16:02.500
The whole affair is in very capable hands.

16:02.500 --> 16:04.500
Well, I just want to know one thing.

16:04.500 --> 16:05.500
I know.

16:05.500 --> 16:08.500
All you're allowed to do is just put on your nicest party dress

16:08.500 --> 16:11.500
and be ready at 827, Thorny Stop and Bye Forest End.

16:11.500 --> 16:14.500
Well, OK, there.

16:14.500 --> 16:15.500
Oh, I'll get it.

16:15.500 --> 16:17.500
It's probably some more of the neighborhood kids.

16:17.500 --> 16:18.500
Oh, all right.

16:18.500 --> 16:19.500
Well, there's some lollipops on the chair there.

16:19.500 --> 16:22.500
Yes, I know.

16:22.500 --> 16:24.500
Trick or treat.

16:24.500 --> 16:26.500
You heard me, trick or treat.

16:26.500 --> 16:28.500
Don't you know this is Halloween?

16:28.500 --> 16:30.500
Oh, yes, but aren't you a little old for this sort of thing?

16:30.500 --> 16:32.500
How old are you anyway?

16:32.500 --> 16:34.500
53.

16:34.500 --> 16:35.500
Ridiculous.

16:35.500 --> 16:38.500
Who ever heard of a full-grown man out trick or treating?

16:38.500 --> 16:41.500
My little boy's over in the next block, and I'm just giving him a hand.

16:41.500 --> 16:43.500
No, but you don't even have a costume.

16:43.500 --> 16:44.500
Naturally.

16:44.500 --> 16:46.500
What do you think I am, a child?

16:46.500 --> 16:48.500
Trick or treat.

16:48.500 --> 16:51.500
OK, I have some lollipops here.

16:51.500 --> 16:52.500
Here you are.

16:52.500 --> 16:53.500
Here's three lollipops.

16:53.500 --> 16:55.500
Only three?

16:55.500 --> 16:56.500
Well, of course.

16:56.500 --> 17:00.500
I mean, there are lots of other children around the neighborhood too, you know.

17:00.500 --> 17:01.500
Hey, they're lemon.

17:01.500 --> 17:04.500
My kid just loves lemon lollipops.

17:04.500 --> 17:05.500
Oh, good.

17:05.500 --> 17:06.500
How old is your little boy?

17:06.500 --> 17:07.500
25.

17:30.500 --> 17:31.500
Hi, Oz.

17:31.500 --> 17:32.500
How do I look?

17:32.500 --> 17:34.500
Terrific, terrific.

17:34.500 --> 17:35.500
Boy, so do you.

17:35.500 --> 17:38.500
That is really terrific.

17:38.500 --> 17:39.500
What happened to the bagpipes?

17:39.500 --> 17:46.500
Well, I was playing them a little while ago, and all of a sudden I had a blowout.

17:46.500 --> 17:47.500
Aren't the tilts a little long?

17:47.500 --> 17:49.500
Should they come all the way down to your ankles like that?

17:49.500 --> 17:50.500
I don't know, Oz.

17:50.500 --> 17:54.500
After all, McTavish is about 6'6", and he's got very long legs.

17:54.500 --> 17:59.500
Let's just say that the new look has finally come to Scotland.

17:59.500 --> 18:00.500
You like my devil's outfit.

18:00.500 --> 18:01.500
Oh, that is sensational.

18:01.500 --> 18:02.500
That's terrific.

18:02.500 --> 18:03.500
I had to go in the closet to put it on.

18:03.500 --> 18:05.500
Harriet doesn't know what I'm wearing.

18:05.500 --> 18:06.500
Yeah, Captain, that's a seed in my yeti.

18:06.500 --> 18:10.500
Boy, I'll be booming for a big surprise.

18:10.500 --> 18:12.500
Harriet, we're ready.

18:12.500 --> 18:14.500
I'll be right down.

18:14.500 --> 18:16.500
Say, Oz, do you mind if we go in your car tonight?

18:16.500 --> 18:18.500
Well, no, any particular reason.

18:18.500 --> 18:19.500
Well, I've got these kilts on.

18:19.500 --> 18:22.500
I'm afraid if I drive, it'll be a little drafty.

18:22.500 --> 18:23.500
Let's keep going with that, guys.

18:23.500 --> 18:24.500
Well, good.

18:24.500 --> 18:25.500
Holy smokes.

18:25.500 --> 18:27.500
Hey, David, where did you see Ponce?

18:27.500 --> 18:28.500
Like this, huh?

18:28.500 --> 18:30.500
Oh, hiya, Mrs. Thornberry.

18:30.500 --> 18:31.500
Oh, you didn't recognize me.

18:31.500 --> 18:33.500
Holy smokes, what's going on in here?

18:33.500 --> 18:35.500
Look, a Mr. Thornberry's dressed up like a woman.

18:35.500 --> 18:37.500
No, no, he's a Mascotchman.

18:37.500 --> 18:39.500
Look, Pop, he looks like the devil.

18:39.500 --> 18:41.500
Yeah, so does Mr. Thornberry.

18:41.500 --> 18:42.500
Oh, yeah.

18:42.500 --> 18:44.500
Oh, that'll be kind of sporty, Pop.

18:44.500 --> 18:47.500
Okay, take it easy now, boys.

18:47.500 --> 18:48.500
Okay, I'm all ready.

18:48.500 --> 18:49.500
Let's go.

18:49.500 --> 18:50.500
What?

18:50.500 --> 18:51.500
Good to see you.

18:51.500 --> 18:52.500
Surprise.

18:52.500 --> 18:53.500
Oh, you've won an outfit.

18:53.500 --> 18:55.500
Tell me, what's the idea of the evening dress?

18:55.500 --> 18:57.500
Oh, these are just extra long kilts.

18:57.500 --> 18:58.500
A Mascotchman.

18:58.500 --> 19:00.500
I didn't know it was a costume party.

19:00.500 --> 19:02.500
Oh, no, don't you remember I told you yesterday,

19:02.500 --> 19:04.500
Thornie and I are the only ones dressing up.

19:04.500 --> 19:05.500
Say, we'd better hurry.

19:05.500 --> 19:06.500
Catherine's waiting next door for us.

19:06.500 --> 19:08.500
Oh, yes, Harriet, we're going to take our car.

19:08.500 --> 19:09.500
Would you get it out of the garage?

19:09.500 --> 19:12.500
I want to go over to Thornie and get a look at Catherine's face

19:12.500 --> 19:13.500
when she sees that outfit.

19:13.500 --> 19:15.500
And if she calls me Harriet, I'm not going.

19:15.500 --> 19:16.500
I'm not either.

19:16.500 --> 19:17.500
We'll meet you right outside, Harriet.

19:17.500 --> 19:18.500
Hey, wait a minute.

19:18.500 --> 19:19.500
Where are we going?

19:19.500 --> 19:21.500
Oh, to the party.

19:21.500 --> 19:24.500
No, I mean, whose house is the party being given at?

19:24.500 --> 19:26.500
You never did tell me.

19:26.500 --> 19:28.500
Well, now, wait a minute.

19:28.500 --> 19:31.500
You mean to say you girls haven't decided where the party's going to be held tonight?

19:31.500 --> 19:33.500
Well, no, you said you and Thornie were going to take care of everything,

19:33.500 --> 19:37.500
and the girls weren't to worry about a thing.

19:37.500 --> 19:40.500
Well, Thornie, didn't you decide where the party was going to be given?

19:40.500 --> 19:41.500
Oh, now, wait a minute.

19:41.500 --> 19:42.500
Don't blame this on me.

19:42.500 --> 19:43.500
What?

19:43.500 --> 19:44.500
You?

19:44.500 --> 19:48.500
Well, Harriet, didn't...

19:48.500 --> 19:51.500
Isn't anybody giving a party tonight?

19:51.500 --> 19:55.500
Somebody always gives a party on Halloween.

19:55.500 --> 19:58.500
Gee, I got the games all planned and everything.

19:58.500 --> 20:00.500
Well, turn around, Oz.

20:00.500 --> 20:03.500
We can still play pin the tail on the donkey.

20:03.500 --> 20:04.500
That's very good.

20:04.500 --> 20:05.500
Well, there's one sure thing.

20:05.500 --> 20:07.500
We can't let the evening go to waste.

20:07.500 --> 20:09.500
Say, wait a minute.

20:09.500 --> 20:10.500
I've got a wonderful idea.

20:10.500 --> 20:11.500
Such as what?

20:11.500 --> 20:13.500
Well, you've heard the slogan, movies are better than ever.

20:13.500 --> 20:14.500
We'll see a movie.

20:14.500 --> 20:15.500
Oh, no, I'm sorry.

20:15.500 --> 20:18.500
I'm not going to any theater with you two guys dressed like that.

20:18.500 --> 20:19.500
Who said anything about a theater?

20:19.500 --> 20:21.500
We'll go to my house.

20:21.500 --> 20:25.500
The Buffalo Cassidy comes on in exactly five minutes.

20:25.500 --> 20:27.500
Hi, how are you?

20:27.500 --> 20:38.500
I'm just picking up this scarf.

20:38.500 --> 20:39.500
Hi there.

20:39.500 --> 20:40.500
Oh, hi.

20:40.500 --> 20:41.500
Hi.

20:41.500 --> 20:42.500
Oh, hi.

20:42.500 --> 20:44.500
Party's going beautifully, isn't it?

20:44.500 --> 20:46.500
Yes, it sure is.

20:46.500 --> 20:48.500
I suppose you think you're pretty smart.

20:48.500 --> 20:50.500
To be honest with you, yes.

20:50.500 --> 20:53.500
I mean, make it believe you didn't know where the party was going to be held.

20:53.500 --> 20:56.500
Well, believe me, I didn't until about four o'clock.

20:56.500 --> 21:00.500
It wasn't until all of us girls started talking it over and discovered that nobody was having one.

21:00.500 --> 21:03.500
It's nice of Katherine to have the party here, though, isn't it?

21:03.500 --> 21:06.500
Yes, it is.

21:06.500 --> 21:10.500
You know, I'll admit that I did overlook one little detail.

21:10.500 --> 21:13.500
But you have to admit that everything's going along real smooth, don't you think?

21:13.500 --> 21:15.500
So everybody having a good time and all?

21:15.500 --> 21:16.500
Well, that's what I said.

21:16.500 --> 21:17.500
It's a wonderful party.

21:17.500 --> 21:18.500
And you know why?

21:18.500 --> 21:20.500
Because everything has been planned.

21:20.500 --> 21:21.500
All the details have been arranged.

21:21.500 --> 21:25.500
A well-planned party is a successful party.

21:25.500 --> 21:28.500
Uh...

21:28.500 --> 21:31.500
Let's see, it's a quarter of twelve.

21:31.500 --> 21:34.500
I think we'll have the refreshments at, uh...

21:34.500 --> 21:36.500
about twelve-thirteen.

21:36.500 --> 21:37.500
Fine.

21:37.500 --> 21:41.500
What are we having for refreshments?

21:41.500 --> 21:43.500
I beg your pardon?

21:43.500 --> 21:45.500
I said, what are we having for refreshments?

21:45.500 --> 21:47.500
All the girls have been asking me.

21:47.500 --> 21:50.500
But don't tell me you girls haven't prepared refreshments.

21:50.500 --> 21:52.500
Why not? This is your party.

21:52.500 --> 21:58.500
Don't tell me you've overlooked another little detail.

21:58.500 --> 22:02.500
I really had you going there for a minute, didn't I?

22:02.500 --> 22:04.500
You don't think we'd overlook the refreshments, do you?

22:04.500 --> 22:07.500
Where are they?

22:07.500 --> 22:08.500
Don't worry, dear.

22:08.500 --> 22:09.500
I'll take care of everything.

22:09.500 --> 22:11.500
You just go ahead and join the folks.

22:11.500 --> 22:13.500
I'm going to take care of a few more details.

22:13.500 --> 22:14.500
Need any help, sir?

22:14.500 --> 22:15.500
No, no, no.

22:15.500 --> 22:17.500
Have a good time, and I'll be right back.

22:17.500 --> 22:46.500
All right, you say so.

22:46.500 --> 22:47.500
Uh, hello?

22:47.500 --> 22:50.500
Uh, Harry's Hamburger Haven.

22:50.500 --> 22:57.500
I'd like to make a reservation for, uh, thirty-one people at, uh...

22:57.500 --> 23:01.500
Uh, uh, twelve-twenty-three, please.

23:01.500 --> 23:03.500
Yes.

23:03.500 --> 23:07.500
With the onions on top.

23:07.500 --> 23:10.500
Tommy, have you seen my gray jacket anyplace?

23:10.500 --> 23:12.500
Oh, yes, it's right there in the living room.

23:12.500 --> 23:13.500
Why, are you going downtown?

23:13.500 --> 23:14.500
Yeah, I'm going to market.

23:14.500 --> 23:18.500
Uh, before you go, you must admit we all had a good time at the party last night.

23:18.500 --> 23:19.500
We certainly did.

23:19.500 --> 23:20.500
Everybody had a wonderful time.

23:20.500 --> 23:22.500
Oh, thanks very much.

23:22.500 --> 23:25.500
Of course, I'll admit we did forget a couple of little details,

23:25.500 --> 23:28.500
but we didn't forget to do the dishes, and we didn't forget to clean up the place.

23:28.500 --> 23:29.500
No, it was very nice of you.

23:29.500 --> 23:33.500
You know, they say that men forget even more than women.

23:33.500 --> 23:35.500
Oh, Jiminy, that reminds me.

23:35.500 --> 23:37.500
I was supposed to meet Clara Randolph downtown.

23:37.500 --> 23:39.500
Oh, that clock must be stopped.

23:39.500 --> 23:40.500
What time is it?

23:40.500 --> 23:42.500
It's, uh...

23:42.500 --> 23:44.500
Where's my watch?

23:44.500 --> 23:47.500
Oh, for goodness sakes, I left it on Thorny's coffee table.

23:47.500 --> 23:49.500
You know, I was timing the charades last night.

23:49.500 --> 23:50.500
I'll get it for you.

23:50.500 --> 23:51.500
Oh.

23:51.500 --> 23:53.500
And that reminds me, I need some money.

23:53.500 --> 23:55.500
I don't know what you'd do without my wallet.

23:55.500 --> 23:57.500
Neither do I.

23:57.500 --> 24:00.500
Wait a minute, where is my wallet?

24:00.500 --> 24:03.500
Oh, remember there were no pockets in the devil's outfit.

24:03.500 --> 24:05.500
I must have left it upstairs on the hall table.

24:05.500 --> 24:07.500
Oh, well, I'll get it for you.

24:07.500 --> 24:10.500
You sure there isn't anything else you've forgotten?

24:10.500 --> 24:14.500
Well, now, wait a minute, let me check.

24:14.500 --> 24:16.500
No, it's still on.

24:16.500 --> 24:17.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:17.500 --> 24:18.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:18.500 --> 24:19.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:19.500 --> 24:20.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:20.500 --> 24:21.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:21.500 --> 24:22.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:22.500 --> 24:23.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:23.500 --> 24:24.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:24.500 --> 24:25.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:25.500 --> 24:26.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:26.500 --> 24:27.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:27.500 --> 24:28.500
Ha, ha, ha.

24:28.500 --> 24:30.500
The Man at the Door was played by William Hayes.

24:30.500 --> 24:34.500
The little children were Jerry Mathers and Vicki Churchill.

24:34.500 --> 24:41.500
The Man at the Door was played by William Hayes.

24:41.500 --> 24:46.500
The Little Children were played by Jerry Mathers and Vicki Churchill.

24:46.500 --> 24:52.500
The Man at the Door was played by William Hayes.

24:52.500 --> 24:58.500
The Little Children were played by Jerry Mathers and Vicki Churchill.

24:58.500 --> 25:05.500
The Man at the Door was played by William Hayes.

25:05.500 --> 25:30.500
The Man at the Door was played by William Hayes.

